You are given an array of n integers. Your task is to calculate the median of each window of k elements, from left to right.
The median is the middle element when the elements are sorted. If the number of elements is even, there are two possible medians and we assume that the median is the smaller of them.

Input:
The first line contains two integers n and k: the number of elements and the size of the window.
Then there are n integers x_1,x_2,...,x_n: the contents of the array.

Output:
Print n-k+1 values: the medians.

Constraints:
1<=k<=n<=2*10^5
1<=x_i<=10^9

Strategy:
Here we use the idea that we can split our input into the lower and upper half. The median will always be at the edge of the bigger one. We can do this be keeping 2 multisets. One for the lower and one for the higher half. Then it is just a matter of adding an element for each iteration [1,n] and for the ones with i > k we have to also remove the element delayed by k to keep the window size. We use the same thing as for the sum keeping a deque and popping with populating it by not popping for the first k iterations. For the multisets we always insert x then if i > k we find where the popped value is and remove that and then we need to balance the multisets to be actual halves (size diff at most 1). We do this by checking the sizes and if the difference is bigger we take an element from the bigger one (lower.rbegin() or higher.begin()) and put it in the other one. This way we keep them halves. For each i >= k we output the right edge representing the absolute middle (lower.rbegin() or higher.begin()). A very important part is using .erase(find(x)) and not just erase since that deletes everything with value x.